permalink: /faq/
seoTitle: "Tampa Bay Fishing Charter FAQ: Prices, Licenses & Kids | CFTB"
description: "Answers to Tampa Bay fishing charter questions: prices, group sizes, start times, licenses, kids, weather, deposits & where trips leave. Call (813) 714-5670."
h1: Tampa Bay Fishing Charter FAQ
intro: "The questions our guests ask most. Still have one? Call or text (813) 714-5670."
faqs:
  - group: Booking and prices
    items:
      - q: How much does a fishing charter cost?
        a: 'Prices are per boat, not per person. Crappie starts at $550. Inshore, redfish, snook and shark start at $600. Nearshore and tarpon start at $650, and offshore at $950. Every price by trip length is on the <a href="/charters/">charters page</a>.'
      - q: How many people can come?
        a: 'Inshore, nearshore, offshore and shark trips take up to six; the price covers four, and the fifth and sixth guests are $75 and $150. Redfish, tarpon and crappie trips take up to four. Snook trips take up to three.'
      - q: What time do trips leave?
        a: 'Morning trips leave at 8:00 AM. Most 4 and 6 hour trips can also leave at 1:00 PM. Crappie trips leave in the morning only.'
      - q: How do I reserve a trip?
        a: 'Book online or call or text (813) 714-5670. A 10% deposit holds your date.'
      - q: What happens if the weather is bad?
        a: 'If conditions are unsafe, you get a new date or your deposit back. Our captains call it straight rather than run a trip that isn''t safe or worth fishing. An <a href="/inshore/">inshore trip</a> inside the bay is often the backup when the Gulf is rough.'
      - q: Do you sell gift cards?
        a: 'Yes, in any amount, good for any trip. Buy them on our booking page.'
  - group: What's included and what to bring
    items:
      - q: Do I need a fishing license?
        a: 'Not for saltwater trips; the license is covered for everyone on board, including the snook permit. <a href="/crappie-fishing/">Crappie trips</a> are freshwater, so each angler brings a Florida freshwater fishing license.'
      - q: What's included?
        a: 'Your captain, rods, reels, tackle and bait, and a cooler with water and ice. On <a href="/offshore-fishing/">offshore trips</a> your catch is cleaned at the dock.'
      - q: What should I bring?
        a: 'Any food and drinks you want other than water, polarized sunglasses, and sun protective clothing or sunscreen.'
  - group: On the water
    items:
      - q: Can kids come?
        a: 'Yes. Inshore, shark and crappie trips are the easiest for young anglers. One guest wrote that Capt. Gonzalez "kept them engaged and excited the whole time" on a trip with his two nephews.'
      - q: Do I need experience?
        a: 'No. Your captain rigs the rods, picks the spot and coaches you through the bite and the fight.'
      - q: What will we catch?
        a: 'Inshore: redfish, snook, trout, Spanish mackerel and snapper. Nearshore: grouper, snapper, grunts, sheepshead and kingfish. Offshore: red grouper, red snapper and other reef fish. Crappie on the lakes. Our <a href="/fishing-reports/">fishing reports</a> show what was biting recently, like the <a href="/fishing-reports/october-2026-fishing-roundup-tampa-bay/">October 2026 roundup</a>.'
      - q: Can we keep fish?
        a: 'Yes, what''s in season and within Florida limits. Tarpon are catch and release, and snook can only be kept in the spring and fall harvest windows.'
  - group: Where we go
    items:
      - q: Where do trips leave from?
        a: 'Capt. Pruner''s inshore trips leave from Ruskin, at 611 Destiny Drive. Offshore trips launch from a ramp around Tampa Bay chosen for the day''s weather, and crappie trips launch on the lake we''re fishing that week. Your captain confirms the meeting point when you book.'
      - q: What areas do you fish?
        a: 'Tampa Bay from Ruskin to St. Petersburg, the Gulf past the mouth of the bay, and the Kissimmee area lakes for crappie. See <a href="/tampa-bay-fishing/">where we fish</a>.'
      - q: Who are the captains?
        a: 'Capt. William Ashmore, Capt. David Pruner, Capt. Frankie Gonzalez and Capt. Rob Hahn, each running his own boat. Meet them on <a href="/about/">Our Captains</a>, read their <a href="/reviews/">reviews</a>, or <a href="/contact-us/">send us your dates</a>.'
---
